IMO-2000-notes.pdf,5,"Does there exist a positive integer n such that n has exactly 2000 distinct prime
divisors and n divides 2n + 1?","Answer: Yes.
We say that n is Korean if n | 2n + 1. First, observe that n = 9 is Korean. Now, the
problem is solved upon the following claim:
Claim --- If n > 3 is Korean, there exists a prime p not dividing n such that np is
Korean too.
Proof. I claim that one can take any primitive prime divisor p of 22n -1, which exists by
Zsigmondy theorem. Obviously p ̸= 2. Then:
{\textbullet} Since p \nmid2\phi(n) -1 it follows then that p \nmidn.
{\textbullet} Moreover, p | 2n + 1 since p \nmid2n -1.
Hence np | 2n + 1 | 2np + 1 by Chinese Theorem, since gcd(n, p) = 1.
