np can be any residue modulo q). Since p \equivnp (mod q) =\Rightarrowpk \equiv1 (mod q), it suffices
to prevent the latter situation from happening.
So we need a prime q \equiv1 (mod p) such that pk ̸\equiv1 (mod q). To do this, we first
recall the following lemma.","Lemma
Let \Phip(X) = 1 + X + X2 + \cdot \cdot \cdot + Xp-1. For any integer a, if q is a prime divisor of
\Phip(a) other than p, then a (mod q) has order p. (In particular, q \equiv1 (mod p).)
Proof. We have ap -1 \equiv0 (mod q), so either the order is 1 or p. If it is 1, then a \equiv1
(mod q), so q | \Phip(1) = p, hence q = p.
Now the idea is to extract a prime factor q from the cyclotomic polynomial
\Phip(p) = pp -1
