IMO-2006-notes.pdf,5,"Let P(x) be a polynomial of degree n > 1 with integer coefficients and let k be a
positive integer. Consider the polynomial
Q(x) = P(P(. . . P(P(x)) . . . ))
where P occurs k times. Prove that there are at most n integers t such that Q(t) = t.
First, we prove that:","Claim (Putnam 2000 et al) --- If a number is periodic under P then in fact it's fixed
by P {\textopenbullet}P.
Proof. Let x1, x2, . . . , xn be a minimal orbit. Then
xi -xi+1 | P(xi) -P(xi+1) = xi+1 -xi+2
and so on cyclically.
If any of the quantities are zero we are done. Else, we must eventually have xi -xi+1 =
-(xi+1 -xi+2), so xi = xi+2 and we get 2-periodicity.
The tricky part is to study the 2-orbits. Suppose there exists a fixed pair u ̸= v with
P(u) = v, P(v) = u. (If no such pair exists, we are already done.) Let (a, b) be any
other pair with P(a) = b, P(b) = a, possibly even a = b, but {a, b} \cap{u, v} = \varnothing. Then
we should have
u -a | P(u) -P(a) = v -b | P(v) -P(b) = u -a
and so u -a and v -b divide each other (and are nonzero). Similarly, u -b and v -a
divide each other.
Hence u -a = \pm(v -b) and u -b = \pm(v -a). We consider all four cases:
{\textbullet} If u -a = v -b and u -b = v -a then u -v = b -a = a -b, contradiction.
{\textbullet} If u -a = -(v -b) and u -b = -(v -a) then u + v = u -v = a + b.
{\textbullet} If u -a = -(v -b) and u -b = v -a, we get a + b = u + v from the first one
(discarding the second).
{\textbullet} If u -a = v -b and u -b = -(v -a), we get a + b = u + v from the second one
(discarding the first one).
Thus in all possible situations we have
a + b = c := u + v
a fixed constant.
Therefore, any pair (a, b) with P(a) = b and P(b) = a actually satisfies P(a) = c -a.
And since deg P > 1, this means there are at most n roots to a + P(a) = c, as needed.
