IMO-2011-notes.pdf,1,"Given any set A = {a1, a2, a3, a4} of four distinct positive integers, we denote the sum
a1 + a2 + a3 + a4 by sA. Let nA denote the number of pairs (i, j) with 1 \lei < j \le4
for which ai + aj divides sA. Find all sets A of four distinct positive integers which
achieve the largest possible value of nA.
There are two curves of solutions, namely {x, 5x, 7x, 11x} and {x, 11x, 19x, 29x}, for any
positive integer x, achieving nA = 4 (easy to check). We'll show that nA \le4 and equality
holds only in one of the curves.
Let A = {a < b < c < d}.","Claim --- We have nA \le4 with equality iff
a + b | c + d,
a + c | b + d,
a + d = b + c.
Proof. Note a + b | sA \Leftarrow\Rightarrowa + b | c + d etc. Now c + d \nmida + b and b + d \nmida + c for size
reasons, so we already have nA \le4; moreover a + d | b + c and b + c | a + d if and only if
a + d = b + c.
We now show the equality curve is the one above.
a + c | b + d \Leftarrow\Rightarrowa + c | -a + 2b + c \Leftarrow\Rightarrowa + c | 2(b -a).
Since a + c > |b -a|, so we must have a + c = 2(b -a). So we now have
c = 2b -3a
d = b + c -a = 3b + c -4a.
The last condition is
a + b | c + d = 5b -7a \Leftarrow\Rightarrowa + b | 12a.
Now, let x = gcd(a, b). The expressions for c and d above imply that x | c, d so we may
scale down so that x = 1. Then gcd(a + b, a) = gcd(a, b) = 1 and so a + b | 12.
We have c > b, so 3a < b. The only pairs (a, b) with 3a < 2b, gcd(a, b) = 1 and
a + b | 12 are (a, b) \in{(1, 5), (1, 11)} which give the solutions earlier.
