Now
f(a)2 + f(b)2 + f(a + b)2 = 2f(a + b) (f(a) + f(b)) + 2f(a)f(b)
or
(f(a + b) -(f(a) + f(b)))2 = 4f(a)f(b).
Hence f(a)f(b) is a perfect square for all a, b \inZ.
So there exists a \lambda such that
f(n) = \lambdag(n)2, where g(n) \ge0. From here we recover
g(a + b) = \pmg(a) \pm g(b) .
