Since we have the bound hk+1 \lehk + C, we are done upon proving the following lower
bound:
Claim --- For every 1 \le\ell< L and n > N', we have h\ell\leh\ell+1 + C \cdot (L -1).
Proof. Assume for contradiction that there is some moment n > N' such that
h\ell> h\ell+1 + C \cdot (L -1)
and WLOG assume that h\ellwas just updated at the moment n. Together with hk+1 \le
hk + C for all k and triangle inequality, we conclude
min(h1, . . . , h\ell) > q := max(h\ell+1, . . . , hL).
We find that the blocks now in fact alternate between being placed among the first \ell
towers and in towers with indices greater than q thereafter. Hence the heights h\ell+1, . . . ,
hL never grow after this moment. This contradicts the definition of L.
Remark. In fact, it can be shown that the period is actually exactly L, meaning the periodic
part will be exactly a permutation of (1, 2, . . . , L). For any L, it turns out there is indeed a
permutation achieving that periodic part.
