JMO-2019-notes.pdf,2,"For which pairs of integers (a, b) do there exist functions f : Z \toZ and g: Z \toZ
obeying
f(g(x)) = x + a
and
g(f(x)) = x + b
for all integers x?
The answer is if a = b or a = -b. In the former case, one can take f(x) \equivx + a and
g(x) \equivx. In the latter case, one can take f(x) \equiv-x + a and g(x) = -x.
Now we prove these are the only possibilities. First:","Claim --- The functions f and g are bijections.
Proof. Surjectivity is obvious. To see injective, note that if f(u) = f(v) then g(f(u)) =
g(f(v)) =\Rightarrowu + b = v + b =\Rightarrowu = v, and similarly for g.
Note also that for any x, we have
f(x + b) = f(g(f(x))) = f(x) + a
g(x + a) = g(f(g(x))) = g(x) + b.
If either a is zero or b is zero, we immediately get the other is zero, and hence done. So
assume ab ̸= 0.
If |b| > |a|, then two of
{f(0), f(1), . . . , f(b -1)}
(mod |a|)
coincide, which together with repeatedly applying the first equation above will then give
a contradiction to injectivity of f. Similarly, if |a| > |b| swapping the roles of f and g
(and a and b) will give a contradiction to injectivity of g. This completes the proof.
Remark. Here is a way to visualize the argument, so one can see pictorially what is going
on. We draw two parallel number lines indexed by Z. Starting from 0, we draw red arrow
from 0 to f(0), and then a blue arrow from f(0) to g(f(0)) = b, and then a red arrow from
b to g(b) = f(0) + a, and so on. These arrows can be extended both directions, leading to
an infinite ""squaretooth"" wave. The following is a picture of an example with a, b > 0.
