JMO-2022-notes.pdf,1,"For which positive integers m does there exist an infinite sequence in Z/mZ which
is both an arithmetic progression and a geometric progression, but is nonconstant?","Answer: m must not be squarefree.
The problem is essentially asking when there exists a nonconstant arithmetic progression
in Z/mZ which is also a geometric progression. Now,
{\textbullet} If m is squarefree, then consider three (s-s, d, s+d) in arithmetic progression. It's
geometric if and only if s2 = (s -d)(s + d) (mod m), meaning d2 \equiv0 (mod m).
Then d \equiv0 (mod m). So any arithmetic progression which is also geometric is
constant in this case.
{\textbullet} Conversely if p2 | m for some prime p, then any arithmetic progression with common
difference m/p is geometric by the same calculation.
