USAMO-1998-notes.pdf,4,"A computer screen shows a 98 \times 98 chessboard, colored in the usual way. One can
select with a mouse any rectangle with sides on the lines of the chessboard and click
the mouse button: as a result, the colors in the selected rectangle switch (black
becomes white, white becomes black). Find, with proof, the minimum number of
mouse clicks needed to make the chessboard all one color.
The answer is 98. One of several possible constructions is to toggle all columns and rows
with even indices.
In the other direction, let n = 98 and suppose that k rectangles are used, none of
which are n \times n (else we may delete it). Then, for any two orthogonally adjacent cells,
the edge between them must be contained in the edge of one of the k rectangles.
We define a gridline to be a line segment that runs in the interior of the board from
one side of the board to the other. Hence there are 2n -2 gridlines exactly. Moreover,
we can classify these rectangles into two types:
{\textbullet} Full length rectangles: these span from one edge of the board to the other. The two
long sides completely cover two gridlines, but the other two sides of the rectangle
do not.
{\textbullet} Partial length rectangles: each of four sides can partially cover ""half a"" gridline.
See illustration below for n = 6.
Full length
Partial length
Since there are 2n-2 gridlines; and each rectangle can cover at most two gridlines in total
(where partial-length rectangles are ""worth 1
2"" on each of the four sides), we immediately
get the bound 2k \ge2n -2, or k \gen -1.
To finish, we prove that:","Claim --- If equality holds and k = n -1, then n is odd.
Proof. If equality holds, then look at the horizontal gridlines and say two gridlines are
related if some rectangle has horizontal edges along both gridlines. (Hence, the graph
has degree either 1 or 2 at each vertex, for equality to hold.) The reader may verify the
resulting graph consists only of even length cycles and single edges, which would mean
n -1 is even.
Hence for n = 98 the answer is indeed 98 as claimed.
