{\textparagraph} Proof of impossibility for a square.
Now we show that k = 1 is impossible (the
tricky part!). Suppose we have a squared dissected into two similar polygons P \simQ. Let
\Gamma be their common boundary. By counting the number of sides of P and Q we see \Gamma
must run from one side of the square to an opposite side (possibly ending at a corner of
the square). We orient the figure so \Gamma runs from north to south, with P to the west and
Q to the east.
P
Q
\Gamma
Let s be the longest length of a segment in \Gamma.
Claim --- The longest side length of P is max(s, 1). Similarly, the longest side
length of Q is max(s, 1) as well.
Proof. The only edges of P not in \Gamma are the west edge of our original square, which has
length 1, and the north/south edges of P (if any), which have length at most 1. An
identical argument works for Q.
It follows the longest sides of P and Q have the same length! Hence the two polygons
are in fact congruent, ending the proof.
