USAMO 2005 Solution Notes
web.evanchen.cc, updated 31 December 2025
This process ends when no numbers remain on the blackboard. The first and second
cases occur at least k + 1 times (the number of entries decreases by a factor of at most
12 each step), and each time \Sigma gets some nonzero digit, which is never changed at later
steps. Therefore \Sigma has sum of digits at least k + 1 as needed.
Remark. The official solutions contain a slicker proof: it turns out that any multiple of
10e -1 has sum of decimal digits at least 9e. However, if one does not know this lemma it
seems nontrivial to imagine coming up with it.
