{\textparagraph} Second solution (projective).
We start with a synthetic observation.
Claim --- The line AC bisects \angle PAD and \angle BAQ.
Proof. Angle chase: \measuredanglePAC = \measuredanglePAE = \measuredanglePBE = \measuredangleCBD = \measuredangleCAD.
There are three ways to finish from here:
{\textbullet} (Michael Kural) Suppose the external bisector of \angle PAD and \angle BAQ meet lines
BC and DC at X and Y . Then
-1 = (GP; XC) = (FD; Y C)
which is enough to imply that XY , GQ, PF are concurrent (by so-called prism
lemma).
{\textbullet} (Daniel Liu) Alternatively, apply the dual Desargues involution theorem to complete
quadrilateral GQFPCM, through the point A. This gives that an involutive pairing
of
(AC, AM) (AP, AQ) (AG, AF).
This is easier to see if we project it onto the line \ellthrough C perpendicular to
AC: let P ', Q', G', F ' be the images of the last four lines, so C is the common
midpoint of F 'Q' and G'P '. Hence the involution swapping G' \leftrightarrowF ' and P ' \leftrightarrowQ'
coincides with negative inversion through C with power \sqrtCP ' \cdot CQ' which implies
that AM \cap\ellis an infinity point, as desired.
