Remark. The official solution replaces the proof for f(x) = cx with Kronecker density.
Here is how it goes.
From f(1) = c \inK, we get f(c) = c2 \inK, et cetera and hence cn \inK. However, I claim
that when c is not a power of 10, some power of c starts with any specified prefix. For
example, there should be an integer n such that cn starts with the digits 98765. And this is
obviously a contradiction to cn \inK.
To see why, note this is equivalent to requiring that
log10(9.8765) \le{n log10 c} < log10(9.8766)
where {x} is the fractional part of x. However, when c is not a power of 10, the number
log10(c) is irrational. And Kronecker density theorem asserts that whenever \alpha is irrational,
the fractional parts {n\alpha} for n \ge0 are actually equally distributed across the entire open
interval (0, 1). In particular, there are n for which the fractional part lie in any interval of
positive length.
