USAMO-2025-notes.pdf,2,"Let n > k \ge1 be integers. Let P(x) \inR[x] be a polynomial of degree n with no
repeated roots and P(0) ̸= 0. Suppose that for any real numbers a0, . . . , ak such
that the polynomial akxk + \cdot \cdot \cdot + a1x + a0 divides P(x), the product a0a1 . . . ak is
zero. Prove that P(x) has a nonreal root.
By considering any k + 1 of the roots of P, we may as well assume WLOG that n = k + 1.
Suppose that P(x) = (x + r1) . . . (x + rn) \inR[x] has P(0) ̸= 0. Then the problem
hypothesis is that each of the n polynomials (of degree n -1) given by
P1(x) = (x + r2)(x + r3)(x + r4) . . . (x + rn)
P2(x) = (x + r1)(x + r3)(x + r4) . . . (x + rn)
P3(x) = (x + r1)(x + r2)(x + r4) . . . (x + rn)
...
Pn(x) = (x + r1)(x + r2)(x + r3) . . . (x + rn-1)
has at least one coefficient equal to zero. (Explicitly, Pi(x) = P(x)
x+ri .) We'll prove that at
least one ri is not real.
Obviously the leading and constant coefficients of each Pi are nonzero, and there are
n -2 other coefficients to choose between. So by pigeonhole principle, we may assume,
say, that P1 and P2 share the position of a zero coefficient, say the xk one, for some
1 \lek < n -1.","Claim --- If P1 and P2 both have xk coefficient equal to zero, then the polynomial
Q(x) = (x + r3)(x + r4) . . . (x + rn)
has two consecutive zero coefficients, namely bk = bk-1 = 0.
Proof. Invoking Vieta formulas, suppose that
Q(x) = xn-2 + bn-3xn-3 + \cdot \cdot \cdot + b0.
(And let bn-2 = 1.) Then the fact that the xk coefficient of P1 and P2 are both zero
means
r1bk + bk-1 = r2bk + bk-1 = 0
and hence that bk = bk-1 = 0 (since the ri are nonzero).
To solve the problem, we use:
Lemma
If F(x) \inR[x] is a polynomial with two consecutive zero coefficients, it cannot have
all distinct real roots.
